        "content": "     1→[\n     2→  {\n     3→    \"title\": \"FTC Settles with Cox Media Group Over Deceptive 'Active Listening' AI Marketing Claims\",\n     4→    \"description\": \"FTC requires Cox Media Group and two other firms to pay nearly $1 million to settle charges they deceived customers about 'Active Listening' AI-powered marketing service. The companies claimed their service listened to consumer conversations through smart devices, but the service did not actually listen in on conversations or use voice data. Instead, they resold email lists from other data brokers at a significant markup. FTC clarified that clicking through mandatory terms of service does not constitute 'opt-in consent' for invasive data collection.\",\n     5→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n     6→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n     7→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/22/ftc-cox-media/\",\n     8→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-22\",\n     9→    \"topics\": [\"privacy\", \"AI ethics\", \"FTC\", \"marketing\", \"smart devices\", \"data privacy\"],\n    10→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n    11→  },\n    12→  {\n    13→    \"title\": \"Datasette Agent: First Release of Extensible AI Assistant\",\n    14→    \"description\": \"Simon Willison announced the first release of Datasette Agent, a new extensible AI assistant for Datasette. This represents the integration of his LLM Python library (developed over 3 years) with Datasette, providing an extensible AI assistant with features like Fly Sprites sandbox for running commands, 'View SQL query' buttons for charts, improved handling of truncated responses, and better permission tracking.\",\n    15→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    16→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    17→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/21/datasette-agent/\",\n    18→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-21\",\n    19→    \"topics\": [\"AI\", \"LLM\", \"Datasette\", \"AI assistants\", \"Python\", \"developer tools\"],\n    20→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    21→  },\n    22→  {\n    23→    \"title\": \"Anthropic Signs $1.25B/Month Compute Deal with SpaceX\",\n    24→    \"description\": \"SpaceX S-1 filing reveals Cloud Services Agreements with Anthropic for access to compute capacity across COLOSSUS and COLOSSUS II. Anthropic agreed to pay $1.25 billion per month through May 2029, with capacity ramping in May and June 2026 at reduced fees. Either party may terminate with 90 days notice.\",\n    25→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    26→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    27→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/20/anthropic-spacex/\",\n    28→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-20\",\n    29→    \"topics\": [\"AI\", \"LLM\", \"Anthropic\", \"SpaceX\", \"compute infrastructure\", \"cloud computing\"],\n    30→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    31→  },\n    32→  {\n    33→    \"title\": \"Google Gemini 3.5 Flash: More Expensive but Google Plans to Use It Everywhere\",\n    34→    \"description\": \"Google released Gemini 3.5 Flash at Google I/O, skipping the preview modifier and going straight to general availability. Google appears to be using it across many key products. Analysis includes discussion of Gemini Spark (Google's OpenClaw competitor), security concerns around prompt injection, enterprise-grade security with isolated VMs, and the transition from open source Gemini CLI to closed source Antigravity CLI.\",\n    35→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    36→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    37→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/19/gemini-3-5-flash/\",\n    38→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-19\",\n    39→    \"topics\": [\"Google\", \"Gemini\", \"LLM\", \"AI\", \"prompt injection\", \"security\"],\n    40→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    41→  },\n    42→  {\n    43→    \"title\": \"The Last Six Months in LLMs in Five Minutes\",\n    44→    \"description\": \"Simon Willison presented a five-minute lightning talk at PyCon US 2026 covering the latest developments in LLMs from the past six months. The presentation uses his annotated presentation tool and provides a comprehensive overview of recent LLM advancements.\",\n    45→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    46→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    47→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/19/llms-five-minutes/\",\n    48→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-19\",\n    49→    \"topics\": [\"LLM\", \"AI\", \"PyCon\", \"presentation\", \"AI developments\"],\n    50→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    51→  },\n    52→  {\n    53→    \"title\": \"UK Government Digital Service Weighs in on NHS Open Source Decision\",\n    54→    \"description\": \"The Government Digital Service (GDS) published 'AI, open code and vulnerability risk in the public sector' on May 14th, responding to the NHS's decision to close down access to their open source repositories. Key recommendation: 'Keep open by default. Making everything private adds additional delivery and policy costs, and can reduce reuse and scrutiny. Openness should remain the default posture, with closure used sparingly and deliberately.' Terence Eden interprets this as a major escalation in civil service terms.\",\n    55→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    56→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    57→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/17/gds-nhs-open-source/\",\n    58→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-17\",\n    59→    \"topics\": [\"open source\", \"security\", \"AI\", \"government\", \"NHS\", \"policy\"],\n    60→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n    61→  },\n    62→  {\n    63→    \"title\": \"OpenClaw's Six Name Changes Since November\",\n    64→    \"description\": \"Analysis of OpenClaw's naming history through Git commits, showing six different names: Warelay → CLAWDIS → CLAWDBOT → Clawdbot → Moltbot → OpenClaw. The detailed timeline shows the evolution from 'WhatsApp Relay CLI' to 'Personal AI Assistant' between November 2025 and January 2026.\",\n    65→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    66→    \"source\": \"Blog\