nd 298 comments about Google's transition from open source Gemini CLI (Apache 2.0 licensed TypeScript) to closed source Antigravity CLI. The discussion covers concerns about Google's changing stance on open source tools for AI development.\",\n    95→    \"author\": \"Hacker News\",\n    96→    \"source\": \"HN\",\n    97→    \"url\": \"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=40894187\",\n    98→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-22\",\n    99→    \"topics\": [\"Google\", \"open source\", \"AI\", \"Antigravity\", \"Gemini\", \"developer tools\"],\n   100→    \"type\": \"discussion\"\n   101→  },\n   102→  {\n   103→    \"title\": \"Multi-Stream LLMs: New Paper on Parallelizing Prompts, Thinking, I/O\",\n   104→    \"description\": \"Hacker News discussion (100 points, 12 comments) about a new arXiv paper on Multi-Stream LLMs that explores parallelizing and separating prompts, thinking, and input/output operations. This represents an architectural approach to improving LLM performance and efficiency.\",\n   105→    \"author\": \"Hacker News\",\n   106→    \"source\": \"HN\",\n   107→    \"url\": \"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=40893897\",\n   108→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-22\",\n   109→    \"topics\": [\"LLM\", \"research\", \"parallelization\", \"architecture\", \"performance\"],\n   110→    \"type\": \"discussion\"\n   111→  },\n   112→  {\n   113→    \"title\": \"Show HN: KVBoost – Chunk-level KV Cache Reuse for HuggingFace (5-48x Faster TTFT)\",\n   114→    \"description\": \"Release of KVBoost, a tool for chunk-level KV cache reuse for HuggingFace models, achieving 5-48x faster Time To First Token (TTFT). This is a significant performance optimization for transformer-based models, reducing latency in LLM inference.\",\n   115→    \"author\": \"Hacker News\",\n   116→    \"source\": \"HN\",\n   117→    \"url\": \"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=40894125\",\n   118→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-22\",\n   119→    \"topics\": [\"LLM\", \"optimization\", \"HuggingFace\", \"performance\", \"KV cache\", \"inference\"],\n   120→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   121→  },\n   122→  {\n   123→    \"title\": \"CODA: Rewriting Transformer Blocks as GEMM-Epilogue Programs\",\n   124→    \"description\": \"arXiv paper discussed on Hacker News (49 points, 3 comments) about rewriting transformer blocks as GEMM-epilogue programs. This represents a systems-level optimization approach for transformer architectures.\",\n   125→    \"author\": \"Hacker News\",\n   126→    \"source\": \"HN\",\n   127→    \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.08871\",\n   128→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-22\",\n   129→    \"topics\": [\"transformers\", \"optimization\", \"GEMM\", \"systems\", \"performance\"],\n   130→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   131→  }\n   132→]\n   133→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"
